<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.10.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00219</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.10.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2837ef3c-ef5f-4bb7-9658-2007715a1241</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><div>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</div>
<para>For questions 1 - 4, use the problem and graph below.</para>
  <blockquote>Andre bought a new bag of cat food. The next day, he opened it to feed his cat. The graph shows how many ounces were left in the bag on the days after it was bought.

<image alt="Line on coordinate plane, origin O. Horizontal axis from 0 to 44 by 4’s, days since bag was bought. Vertical axis 0 to 64 by 8’s, ounces of cat food. Line passes through 0 comma 56 and 24 comma 16." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="238" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8a41839540c92dd378bcb27baf29707c44cd53ac" width="315"/></blockquote>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated"> 
    <item>How many ounces of food were in the bag 12 days after Andre bought it?  </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> 36. When \(x\) is 12, the \(y\) value is 36. </para><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated"> 
    <item> How many days did it take for the bag to contain 16 ounces of food?</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>24. When \(y\) is 16, the \(x\) value is 24.</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated"> 
    <item>How much did the bag weigh before it was opened?.</item>
  </list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>56. When \(x\) is 0, the \(y\) value is 56.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated"> 
    <item>Approximately many days did it take for the bag to be empty?</item>
</list>
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>33. When \(y\) is 0, the \(x\) value is 33.</para><para>For questsions 5 - 9, use the problem and graph below.</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>A group of hikers is progressing steadily along an uphill trail. The graph shows their elevation (or height above sea level), in feet, at each distance from the start of the trail, in miles.</para>
  <div><image alt="Graph of a line. Elevation, feet. Distance along trail, miles." height="179" role="image" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b8eb3dec29ba5c5c9029dfa3cbe813a4df970361" title="mamS5H5Jno6yyY8XAKvjyoLY" width="248"/></div>   
  
</blockquote>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated"> 
  <item>What is the slope of the graph?. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>250. Use points \((0,500)\) and \((2,1000)\)</para> 
<para> \(m=\frac{1000-500}{2-1}=\frac{500}{2}=250\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated"> 
  <item>What does the slope tell us about this situation?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>The change in elevation as the hikers hike more miles.</item>
  <item>The change in miles as the elevation increases.</item>
  <item>Only the distance along the trial (miles).</item>
  <item>Only the elevation (feet).</item>  
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:  </emphasis>The change in elevation as the hikers hike more miles<emphasis/></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated"> 
      <item>Choose the
    equation that represents the relationship between the hikers' distance from the
    start of the trail, \(x\), and their elevation, \(y\).</item></list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(y=250x+500\). The slope is 250. The \(y\)-intercept, when \(x=0\), is 500.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>A kindergarten teacher bought $21 worth of stickers and cardstock for his class. The stickers cost $1.50 a sheet, and the cardstock cost $3.50 per pack. The equation \( 1.5s+3.5c=21 \) represents the relationship between sheets of stickers, \(s\), packs of cardstock, \(c\), and the dollar amount the kindergarten teacher spent on these supplies. 
  <image alt="Line on grid, origin O. Horizontal axis, 0 to 18 by 2’s, sheets of stickers. Vertical axis, 0 to 10 by 2’s, packs of cardstock. Line passes through points 0 comma 6, 7 comma 3, and 14 comma 0." height="140" role="image" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9ab02a96ee7d70b167808f620e20c29384caa813" title="kehVi8A4H5nQTNwBQqGvLNa9" width="239"/></item>
</list>  
  <para>What does the vertical intercept, (0,6), mean in this situation?</para>
<list>
  <item>0 sheets of stickers and 6 packs of cardstock were bought.</item>
  <item>6 sheets of stickers and 0 packs of cardstock were bought.</item>
  <item>7 sheets of stickers and 3 packs of cardstock were bought.</item>
  <item>14 sheets of stickers and 6 packs of cardstock were bought.</item>

</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>0 sheets of stickers and 6 packs of cardstock were bought.</para></section>
